use std
into meshlet.amni

Namespace meshlet; imports math. Splits a triangle mesh into clusters of at most 128 triangles and builds coarser versions of it as levels of cluster groups, so a renderer can pick, cluster by cluster, the coarsest triangles whose error the view cannot show, with no cracks between neighbors. Coarser levels use the mesh's own vertices plus new ones that `simplify` writes after them in the same vertex array, so every level indexes one vertex buffer. Vertex positions come as `pos`, an f32 slice, with `stride` floats per vertex and the x, y, z of vertex v at `pos[v * stride + at]`, `+ 1`, `+ 2`. All geometry math is done in `real`; results stored in tables are `f32`.

Module constants: `MAX_TRIS = 128`, `GROUP = 8`, `GROUP_MAX = 16`, `SOLO = 256`, `ROW = 20`, `SAMPLES = 128`, `FAR f32 = 1e30`. Module vars (outputs, valid after `build`): `idx`, a growable u32 array (the triangles of every cluster, three vertex ids each, cluster after cluster), `tab`, a growable f32 array (`ROW` floats per cluster), `rng`, a growable u32 array (two per cluster: where its triangles start in `idx`, and how many index values it has), `count int` (clusters), `leaves int` (clusters of level 0), `levels int`, `level_tris`, a growable int array (triangles made at each level), `weld`, a growable int array (per vertex: the smallest vertex id at the same place), `smp`, a growable int array (sample vertex ids, cluster after cluster: vertices of the original mesh that the cluster stands for), `srng`, a growable int array (two per cluster: where its samples start in `smp`, and how many it has), `next_v int` (the id the next new vertex gets; after `build`, the number of vertices in use). The row of cluster c starts at `tab[c * ROW]`: 0..2 the center of its bounding sphere, 3 its radius; 4..6 the axis of its normal cone, 7 the cone cutoff; 8..10 the center of its error sphere, 11 that sphere's radius; 12..14 the center of its parent group's error sphere, 15 that radius; 16 its own error; 17 its parent group's error (`FAR` when it has no parent); 18 its level; 19 zero.

`weld_build(pos, stride int, at int, nv int)` (pos: read-only f32 slice) fills `weld` (length nv). Two vertices are at the same place when their coordinates, each taken relative to the mesh's lowest corner, divided by `max(largest side of the mesh's bounding box, 1e-30) / 1048576.0` and rounded to the nearest int, are equal on all three axes. Pack the three ints into one int key (`(qx << 42) | (qy << 21) | qz`) and use an open-addressing hash table (two int arrays for keys and vertex ids, size the smallest power of two at least `2 * nv + 16`, empty slots marked by vertex id -1, linear probing, hash `(key * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 as wrapping multiply or any good integer mix) & (size - 1)` made non-negative). Visiting vertices in increasing id, a vertex whose key is already in the table gets that entry's vertex id, otherwise it is entered and welds to itself.

`sort_by_key(keys, order)` (keys: read-only int slice; order: growable int array by reference) fills `order` with 0..keys.len-1 sorted by key ascending, stable, where every key is in 0..2^30-1: an LSD radix sort in three passes of 10 bits (counting sort with 1024 buckets per pass, swapping between `order` and a temporary array).

`nearest(samples, tris, pos, stride int, at int, near, dist) real` (samples: read-only int slice of vertex ids; tris: read-only int slice, three vertex ids per triangle, at least one triangle; pos: read-only f32 slice; near: growable int array by reference; dist: growable real array by reference) finds, for every sample, the triangle of the list closest to the sample vertex's position: `near` gets that triangle's number in the list and `dist` the distance (both cleared first, one entry per sample). It returns the largest of those distances, 0.0 with no samples. The distance from a point to a triangle is the distance to the closest point of the triangle (inside it, on an edge or at a corner), found with the usual region tests on the point's projections onto the edges; never divide by zero (a triangle with no area still gives the distance to its closest corner or edge). To go fast, first compute every triangle's centroid and the largest distance from it to one of its corners (its radius; keep the largest radius of all, R), pick the axis along which the centroids spread most (largest minus smallest centroid coordinate), and sort the triangle numbers by centroid coordinate on that axis (any correct sort, n log n or better; lists reach 20,000 triangles). For each sample with coordinate c on that axis, binary-search the sorted list for the first centroid coordinate not below c, then walk upward from there while the centroid coordinate minus c minus R is below the best distance found so far, and downward from just before it while c minus the centroid coordinate minus R is below the best distance; in both walks skip a triangle when the distance from the sample to its centroid minus its own radius is not below the best distance found so far.

`emit(tris, pos, stride int, at int, level int, err f32, own bool, lx f32, ly f32, lz f32, lr f32, samples, near, dist, made)` (tris: read-only int slice; pos: read-only f32 slice; samples and near: read-only int slices; dist: read-only real slice; made: growable int array by reference) turns a triangle list (`tris`: three vertex ids per triangle, at least one triangle) into clusters appended to the module tables, appending each new cluster's id to `made`, and adds the triangle count to `level_tris[level]` (growing `level_tris` with zeros as needed).
- Order the triangles along a Morton curve: each triangle's centroid, relative to the lowest corner of the bounding box of all the list's centroids and divided by that box's largest side (at least 1e-30), gives three numbers in 0..1; multiply by 1023, truncate to int, clamp to 0..1023, and interleave the bits of the three (x in bit 0, y in bit 1, z in bit 2 of each triple) into a 30-bit key. Sort with `sort_by_key`.
- With n triangles, make `k = (n + MAX_TRIS - 1) / MAX_TRIS` clusters: cluster j takes the sorted triangles from `j * n / k` up to but not including `(j + 1) * n / k`.
- For each cluster: append its vertex ids to `idx`; its bounding sphere has its center at the middle of the bounding box of its triangles' vertices and the radius reaching the farthest of those vertices; the cone axis is the normalized sum of its triangles' cross-product normals (the edge from v0 to v1 cross the edge from v0 to v2, so longer by area), or (0, 1, 0) when that sum is shorter than 1e-30; with `c` the smallest dot product of the axis with any triangle's unit normal (triangles with a cross product shorter than 1e-30 are skipped), the cutoff is 2.0 when `c <= 0.1`, else `sqrt(1 - c * c)`.
- Samples: append each cluster's samples to `smp` and their start and count to `srng`. When `own` is true, `samples`, `near` and `dist` are ignored and the cluster's samples are the distinct `weld` ids of its vertices in order of first appearance, or, when there are more than `SAMPLES` of them (say m), the ones at positions `i * m / SAMPLES` for i from 0 up to `SAMPLES - 1`. Otherwise sample number s (vertex id `samples[s]`) goes to the cluster that received triangle number `near[s]` of `tris` (numbered as passed in, before sorting); a cluster given more than `SAMPLES` of them keeps the 32 with the largest `dist` and, from the rest in their given order (say m of them), the ones at positions `i * m / 96` for i from 0 up to 95.
- The row's error is `err`; its error sphere is the cluster's own bounding sphere when `own` is true, otherwise (lx, ly, lz) with radius lr; the parent sphere starts as zeros with parent error `FAR`; the level is stored as a float.

`simplify(tris, pos, stride int, at int, target int, out) real` (tris: read-only int slice; pos: writable f32 slice; out: growable int array by reference) reduces a triangle list (three vertex ids per triangle, a few thousand triangles at most) toward `target` triangles by collapsing edges, writes the surviving triangles to `out` (cleared first; three vertex ids each), and returns the largest error of any collapse it made (0.0 when none). It needs `weld` filled. A collapse may make new vertices: it writes their `stride` floats into `pos` at the id taken from `next_v` (which then grows by one) and sets their `weld` entry. Words used below: a triangle corner has an *original* id (its vertex id, from `tris` or a new one) and a *place* (the weld of the id from `tris`, renumbered locally 0..m-1 in order of first appearance; use a module-level int array as a map from weld id to local number, entries reset to -1 after the call so it can be reused, grown to cover the largest id seen). A place's position starts as the position of the first original id seen at it.
- Locked places: count, over all triangles, how often each undirected pair of places is an edge; both places of an edge used by exactly one triangle are locked (use a small open-addressing hash table or sort).
- Quadrics: every place has ten `real` numbers (the upper triangle of a symmetric 4x4 matrix) and a weight. Each triangle with area A > 0, unit normal n and plane offset d (minus the dot product of n with its first corner) adds A times the outer product of (nx, ny, nz, d) with itself to all three of its places' matrices, and A to their weights.
- Partners: for places a and b joined by an edge, the partner at b of an original id `o` used at place a is the original id at place b of a live triangle that has `o` at place a and touches b. The pairing from a to b is *whole* when every distinct original id that live triangles use at a has such a triangle and all such triangles agree on the partner.
- Work in passes, at most 64, stopping as soon as the number of live triangles is at most `target` or a pass makes no collapse. In each pass, first build for every place the list of live triangles touching it. Then list candidate collapses for each ordered pair of places (a, b) that are the two ends of an edge of a live triangle, keeping a candidate only when all of these hold: place a is not locked; the pairing from a to b is whole; (link) the number of distinct places that share a live triangle with a and also share a live triangle with b equals the number of live triangles touching both a and b.
- A candidate *moves* when also b is not locked, the pairing from b to a is whole, and the two pairings are each other's inverse and one-to-one (distinct ids at a have distinct partners at b, every id used at b is the partner of exactly one id at a, and the partner at a of that id's partner is the id itself); otherwise it *snaps*. A moving pair is listed once, from the lower place number.
- Target point p: a snap puts it at b's position. A move puts it on the segment from a's position pa to b's position pb where the summed matrices Q of the two places give the least error: with d = pb minus pa, A the upper-left 3x3 part of Q and v the first three numbers of Q's fourth column, t is minus (the dot product of d with A times pa, plus the dot product of v with d), divided by the dot product of d with A times d; clamp t to 0..1; when that divisor is below 1e-300 use t = 0.5; p is pa plus t times d.
- No flips: every live triangle that touches a or b but not both (for a snap: a but not b), with its corner at a or b put at p, must have a cross-product normal n1 with a dot product with its normal before, n0, above 0.2 times the product of their lengths, and n1 longer than 1e-30. A candidate failing this is dropped.
- The candidate's cost is the error Q gives at p (p extended by 1, multiplied on both sides of Q), divided by the sum of the two weights (at least 1e-30), clamped to at least 0.
- Sort the pass's candidates by cost ascending (any correct sort; there can be several thousand), then take them in that order, looking only at the first K of them, K being half the number of triangles still to remove (live count minus target, plus one, integer-divided by two), and at least 4. Skip a candidate when its place a or place b has been marked touched in this pass. Otherwise apply it and raise the returned error to the square root of its cost when that is larger. Every live triangle touching both a and b dies. A snap: every other live triangle touching a has that corner's place changed to b and its original id changed to the partner of its original id. A move: for each pair of an original id at a and its partner at b, make one new vertex whose floats are (1 - t) times those of the id at a plus t times those of its partner; the first new id made by this collapse is the weld of all the collapse's new vertices; every surviving corner at a or b gets the new id of its pair, corners at a change place to b, and place b's position becomes p. In both cases add a's matrix and weight to b's, and mark as touched a, b, and every place of every triangle that touched a or b before the collapse. Stop the pass early when the live count reaches `target`.
- A triangle that ends up with two corners at the same place is dead.

`group(cur, groups, gfirst)` (cur: read-only int slice; groups and gfirst: growable int arrays by reference) partitions the clusters listed in `cur` into groups of neighbors, or, when `cur` has at most `SOLO` clusters, into a single group holding all of them in order (then nothing below runs): `groups` gets the cluster ids group after group, and `gfirst` the start of each group in `groups` plus one final entry equal to `groups.len` (both cleared first).
- Neighbors: for each cluster find its border edges, the undirected pairs of weld ids (`weld` of the vertex ids in `idx`) used by exactly one of its triangles. Enter each border edge into an open-addressing hash table keyed by `min * 4294967296 + max` that remembers the position in `cur` of the cluster that entered it; when another cluster brings the same edge, the two clusters share one more edge. Keep for every cluster up to 16 neighbor slots (neighbor position in `cur`, shared-edge count); when the slots are full further new neighbors are ignored.
- Greedy pass over `cur` in order: a cluster not yet in a group starts a new one; then, up to `GROUP - 1` times, add the ungrouped cluster with the largest total of shared edges with the group's members (ties: the lowest position), stopping when there is none.
- Then every group that ended with a single cluster joins the group, among those holding one of its neighbors and having fewer than `GROUP_MAX` members, that it shares the most edges with, if there is one (a group that has been joined by others, or has joined another, does not move again).
- Groups are written in order of their first member's position in `cur`; emptied groups are left out.

`build(pos, stride int, at int, nv int, indices)` (pos: writable f32 slice, longer than the mesh needs: its length divided by stride is the capacity in vertices, and the room after the first nv is for new vertices; indices: read-only u32 slice) builds everything for a mesh of nv vertices whose triangles are `indices` (three per triangle, values below nv).
- Clear the module outputs, set `next_v` to nv, run `weld_build`, grow `weld` to the capacity with every added entry equal to its own index, and collect the triangles, dropping any with two corners at the same weld. With none left, set `count`, `leaves` and `levels` to 0 and return.
- Level 0: `emit` them all with level 0, error 0 and `own` true (empty sample arguments). `leaves` is the number made. They are the current level.
- Then repeat, in passes numbered from 1, while the current list has more than one cluster and fewer than 40 passes have run: `group` the current clusters. For each group, gather the triangles of its clusters from `idx`, remember `next_v`, and `simplify` them toward half as many (integer division); a group with more triangles than the capacity has free vertices left (capacity minus `next_v`) is not simplified and has failed. If more than three quarters of the triangles are left (`left * 4 > before * 3`), the group has failed and `next_v` goes back to the remembered value. A failed group: its clusters get no parent in this pass and are carried, unchanged, into the next current list, where they can be grouped again with other neighbors. Otherwise gather the group's samples (its members' samples from `smp`, one member after another) and run `nearest` on them against the simplified triangles; the group's error is the largest distance it returns (as f32), raised if needed to at least the largest error of its member clusters times 1.0001 plus 1e-12; the group's error sphere has its center at the middle of the bounding box of its members' error spheres (each taken as center plus and minus radius) and a radius of the largest `distance(center, member center) + member radius`, times 1.0001; write that sphere and error into the parent fields of every member's row; and `emit` the simplified triangles with the pass number as their level, that error, `own` false and that sphere, passing the group's samples and what `nearest` filled. The next current list is the clusters made by all groups of the pass followed by the carried clusters in their old order; stop when no group of a pass succeeded.
- At the end `count` is the number of clusters and `levels` the number of levels that have clusters.

`cut(e f32, out)` (out: growable u32 array by reference) clears `out` and appends the index values of every cluster whose own error is at most e and whose parent error is above e, in cluster order: the mesh at world-space error e.

`cut_tris(e f32) int` is the number of triangles `cut` would give, without building the list.
